	<p>Sanya stepped into her family’s sprawling home, her heels clicking softly against the marble floor. The moment she entered, she knew what was coming.</p>
 
<p>“Sanya, beta, you’re back.” Her mother, Mrs. Malhotra, looked up from the sofa where she was flipping through a glossy wedding magazine. “Did Vivaan eat properly?”</p>
 
<p>Sanya sighed inwardly, dropping her handbag on the console table. “Yes, Maa.”</p>
 
<p>“And? How was lunch? Did you two talk?”</p>
 
<p>“It was fine.”</p>
 
<p>Her mother’s eyes narrowed slightly. “Just fine?”</p>
 
<p>Sanya pressed her lips together, already feeling the irritation bubble inside her. Here we go again.</p>
 
<p>Mrs. Malhotra set the magazine down and folded her hands in her lap. “Sanya, I don’t understand you. You’re engaged to a man like Vivaan—any girl would be thrilled! But you… you don’t even seem interested.”</p>
 
<p>Sanya exhaled sharply, rolling her eyes. “Maa, please. I went there, I took him lunch, we talked. What more do you want?”</p>
 
<p>“I want you to try!” Her mother’s voice sharpened. “This is your future, Sanya. A good marriage, a stable life. You have everything you need to make this work, but you’re acting as if you don’t even care.”</p>
 
<p>Sanya’s patience snapped. She turned to face her mother, crossing her arms. “Because I don’t care, Maa! I don’t care about being the ‘perfect wife.’ I don’t care about this marriage. And I don’t understand why you keep pushing me into it.”</p>
 
<p>Mrs. Malhotra stood up, her face a mixture of frustration and disbelief. “Then what do you care about? Do you even know what you want to do with your life? You don’t have a career, you have no ambitions, no real interest in marriage or even planning a family. So tell me, Sanya, what do you want?”</p>
 
<p>Sanya opened her mouth to reply, but nothing came out. Because for once, she didn’t have a clever comeback, or an excuse, or an explanation.</p>
 
<p>She didn’t know.</p>
 
<p>She really didn’t know.</p>
 
<p>The realization hit her harder than she expected. Her shoulders slumped slightly, and in a quieter voice, she muttered, “I don’t know, Maa. Just… stop asking me all the time.”</p>
 
<p>Her mother stared at her for a long moment, disappointment evident in her eyes. But instead of arguing further, she sighed and sat back down. “Sanya… you can’t live like this forever. One day, you’ll have to figure it out.”</p>
 
<p>Sanya swallowed hard, looking away.</p>
 
<p>She knew that.</p>
 
<p>She just didn’t know how.</p>
 
<p>The car ride was quiet. Rhea looked out of the window, her fingers tapping lightly on her lap.</p>
 
<p>"You sure you want to do this alone?" Vivaan's voice broke the silence.</p>
 
<p>She turned to him, a small smile on her lips. "Yes. But thanks for coming with me."</p>
 
<p>Vivaan nodded, though he didn’t seem convinced. As the car pulled up to her grandfather’s house, he parked at a distance.</p>
 
<p>"I'll wait here," he said.</p>
 
<p>Rhea didn't argue. She stepped out, adjusted her blazer, and walked in.</p>
 
<p>Dilip was already at the dining table, finishing his lunch. He glanced up when she entered.</p>
 
<p>"Rhea," he greeted, his tone neutral.</p>
 
<p>"Grandpa," she responded, sitting across from him. She placed her phone aside and took a sip of water before adding, "How have you been?"</p>
 
<p>Dilip wiped his hands with a napkin. "Busy, as always. And you?"</p>
 
<p>"The same," she said. "Though I doubt I'm as busy as you."</p>
 
<p>He scoffed. "I highly doubt that. You're running a company now."</p>
 
<p>She smiled faintly. "Yes, I am."</p>
 
<p>A few moments of silence settled between them. This was how it always was—formal, structured. Rhea had learned long ago that with her grandfather, there was always a clear divide: family and business. She decided to keep it that way.</p>
 
<p>She leaned back. "I actually came to talk about something important."</p>
 
<p>Dilip folded his hands, waiting.</p>
 
<p>"The campaign," she continued. "Why haven't you approved it? Every time we bring it up, you push it back."</p>
 
<p>Dilip sighed, shaking his head slightly. "Because it's the same idea you presented before, Rhea. And the last time, it didn’t work."</p>
 
<p>Rhea stiffened. "It’s not the same. We’ve refined it, analyzed the failures, and rebuilt the strategy. This time, it will work."</p>
 
<p>Dilip leaned back, assessing her. "That’s what you said last time."</p>
 
<p>Frustration bubbled inside her, but she kept her tone steady. "Grandpa, you saw what happened in the boardroom. You saw that I stood my ground. Why won’t you trust me on this?"</p>
 
<p>Dilip exhaled. "I see that you’re determined."</p>
 
<p>Rhea blinked, caught off guard. It wasn’t a compliment, but coming from him, it almost felt like one.</p>
 
<p>He set his napkin aside. "Fine. Do it. Let’s see if your campaign succeeds."</p>
 
<p>She frowned. "Just like that?"</p>
 
<p>Dilip’s gaze sharpened. "Not just like that. If this fails, you step down as CEO."</p>
 
<p>Rhea’s breath hitched. "What?"</p>
 
<p>"You heard me," he said. "I will manage the company finances as CFO. You will run the campaign, but if it doesn’t work—if it damages the company further—you will step down, no questions asked."</p>
 
<p>She clenched her fists under the table. He was giving her a chance, but it came with a price.</p>
 
<p>She inhaled deeply. "Fine. I accept."</p>
 
<p>Dilip nodded, satisfied. "Good."</p>
 
<p>Rhea stood up, her mind racing. As she turned to leave, she hesitated. "Grandpa," she said, her voice quieter.</p>
 
<p>He looked up.</p>
 
<p>"Thank you."</p>
 
<p>His expression softened—just a little. "Prove me wrong, Rhea."</p>
 
<p>She nodded and walked out.</p>
 
<p>Vivaan was leaning against the car, arms crossed. He straightened when she approached.</p>
 
<p>"How did it go?"</p>
 
<p>She let out a breath. "He approved the campaign."</p>
 
<p>Vivaan raised a brow. "That easy?"</p>
 
<p>Rhea gave a humorless chuckle. "No. If it fails, I lose my position as CEO."</p>
 
<p>Vivaan’s jaw tightened. He studied her face for a moment before saying, "Then let’s make sure it doesn’t fail."</p>
 
<p>She met his gaze and, for the first time in a long while, felt something solid beneath her feet.</p>
 
<p>"Yeah," she said. "Let's."</p>

<p>The ride back to the office was mostly silent. Rhea sat in the passenger seat, staring out the window, her fingers drumming lightly against her lap. Vivaan didn’t push her to talk—he knew that if she wanted to, she would.</p>
<p>But when they stepped into her office, he finally broke the silence.</p>

<p>"So... how do you feel?" he asked, closing the door behind them.</p>

<p>Rhea sighed and sat on the couch, leaning back against the cushions. "I expected more of a fight. It almost felt... easy. Too easy."</p>

<p>Vivaan leaned against the desk, arms crossed. "Maybe because he sees how determined you are?"</p>

<p>Rhea let out a dry chuckle. "Or maybe he's just waiting for me to fail."</p>

<p>Vivaan studied her for a moment, then walked over, sitting next to her. "You don’t actually believe that, do you?"</p>

<p>She hesitated before shaking her head. "No. He’s just... tough. He doesn’t believe in me as a leader, but he still loves me."</p>

<p>Vivaan tilted his head. "Why doesn’t he believe in you?"</p>

<p>Rhea exhaled slowly, running a hand through her hair. "Because he’s seen failure before. My father made a mistake once—lost millions. He thinks I’m too much like him. Too emotional, too willing to take risks. And in business, emotion is dangerous."</p>

<p>Vivaan frowned. "But that was your father’s mistake, not yours. You’ve already proved you can handle yourself."</p>

<p>She gave him a small, tired smile. "That’s not how he sees it. He thinks I’ll repeat history."</p>

<p>Vivaan studied her carefully before asking the next question. "Is it because you're a girl?"</p>

<p>Rhea shook her head immediately. "No. If that were the case, he wouldn't have supported me all these years. He just... thinks emotions get in the way of good decisions. And maybe he’s right. Maybe I am too emotional."</p>

<p>She paused for a second before adding, "But I don’t care. This isn’t just about me. It was my father’s wish to see me as CEO. He believed in me. And I won’t let him down."</p>

<p>Vivaan leaned in slightly, his voice lower now. "You’re doing great, Rhea. You’re strong, you're smart, and you're leading this company in a way no one else could. Even your grandfather sees that—he just won’t say it."</p>

<p>She looked at him then, really looked at him. His eyes held nothing but honesty, warmth.</p>

<p>And for the first time in a long time, she felt understood. The air between them shifted.</p>

<p>Vivaan reached out, his fingers brushing against her hand. It was a small touch, barely there, but it sent a wave of warmth through her. She didn’t move away.</p>

<p>He lifted his hand, almost without thinking, and tucked a stray strand of hair behind her ear.</p>

<p>Rhea’s breath hitched.</p>

<p>The room felt smaller. The air heavier. His face was so close—too close.</p>

<p>Vivaan’s gaze flickered to her lips for a fraction of a second.</p>

<p>Rhea parted her lips slightly, as if she was about to say something—but no words came.</p>

<p>Their breathing synced.</p>

<p>It would take nothing—just an inch, just a second—for this moment to turn into something irreversible.</p>

<p>Then, as if realizing what was happening, Vivaan pulled back suddenly, his jaw tightening. He stood up quickly, shoving his hands into his pockets.</p>

<p>"I should go."</p>
<p>Rhea blinked, snapping out of whatever trance they’d both been in. She straightened, her heart hammering against her ribs.</p>

<p>"Yeah. You should."</p>

<p>Neither of them moved for a second. Then, without another word, Vivaan turned and left.</p>

<p>Rhea stayed where she was, staring at the door long after he was gone.</p>

<p>Her fingers lightly touched the spot where he had tucked her hair away.</p>

<p>What just happened?</p>



        
